From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: Re:  CORP: shareholder interests

> All economic research indicates that it is impossible to create a monopoly
> without government coercion.

> Krzys'

We shouldn't overlook the concept of "natural monopoly."  Sometimes a market
niche is only large enough to support one firm big enough to have the
necessary economy of scale to compete.  As long as this firm's prices remain
reasonable, it can maintain its monopoly status indefinitely.  As soon as it
engages in "predatory pricing," it opens itself up to competition.  Its
customers, feeling abused, will be likely to use this new competitor, which
means the new firm will become the natural monopoly.

As far as creating a monopoly which can use predatory pricing and still keep
its market, Krzys' is correct.  These firms survive only because the State
protects them from competition by force.

