Date:     Fri, 22 Jan 93 10:32:01 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Diamond Mine Monopolies

First of all, I think that some are being too argumentative in their
criticism of David Jevans' claims (this means you, Perry, Kris).  Please
try to stick to being informative instead in the interest of elevating
the discussion level.

Secondly, though I must add a note of criticism to his example of the
diamond mine monopoly.  There are two kinds of monopolies: natural, and
coercive.  The difference is that coercive ones are the result of coercive
restraint against actual or potential competitors.

We live in a world in which we presently have a diamond cartel, a form of
monopoly, De Beers.  Many believe it to be a natural monopoly (a libertarian
friend of mine was told in his econ class at George Washington U that it
was one of the few in the world at this time), falsely.  In South Africa,
for instance, freelance diamond mining is prohibited.  De Beers is a coercive
monopoly.  It is not a market failure, but a public policy failure.

It's also in big trouble, but that's another story.

