Date: Fri, 22 Jan 1993 14:12:23 -0800
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: Information density of hi-fi VR

I think the following quotes represent a misjudgement of the
complexity of sensory experience, especially when compared to the 
complexity of our brains' processing...

>> But I do think there are questions as to whether a certain volume of 
>> computers can simulate as much virtual space as the physical space
>> they take up...It's only by reducing the resolution of the
>> simulation that we can provide a VR with adequate volume.
> 
>> Hal Finney
>
>Regarding your point as to whether VR can ever be as rich as physical
>reality, there appears to me to be a tradeoff between detail of experience
>and rapidity of thought.  If you want to experience a walk through the
>forest followed by a gourmet meal, while I want to skip all the frills and
>just stick to business, it's going to take you longer than me to have our
>respective experiences.  (As an analogy, my ink-jet printer gives me a
>choice between "fast" and "best.")
> 
>Mark Read Pickens   autarch@well.sf.ca.us

Have these guys done their arithmetic?

1) For VR, almost all the detail you would want to store is *surfaces* of 
things, at up-close human-eye resolution.  Even current DRAMs are getting 
close to the density needed for that.  Figure 24 bits x 1000 dpi.
That's 24 million bits per square inch.  A 4 Meg x 8 SIMM has maybe
8 square inches of area--32 million bits/8 sq.in. = 4 million bits/sq.in.
Or consider an 8mm tape--16 billion bits/16 sq.in. = 1 billion bits/sq.in.

2) To upload a brain requires storage of detail through its whole volume.
10^15 synapses = 159 acres at 1 million synapes/sq.in.  

3) Processing power required is roughly the same per storage byte for VR
and brains.  Ray tracing might require a little more processing per pixel
than brains do per synapse, but at a slower rate (eye response rates vs. 
neuron firing rates).  This is true however fast you live.  So,

-->   The storage and processing cost of a modern person's mind could 
      easily exceed that of a hi-fi VR of all their lands and property.

4) I doubt it would stretch the capacity of a nanocomputer the volume of 
a brain.  I imagine tens of bytes per synapse and a processor per neuron 
for human-equivalent speed (number of processors goes up with speed).
No sweat.

All that remains is nanotech and software. >:o)

