From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: Re: Nutrition

Perry Metzger writes:
>I believe the advice has been consistant for many years now. At the very
>least, eat a low fat diet with a decent supply of fiber. The links between
>high fat intake and heart disease, stroke, several kinds of cancers (including
>colon cancer) and other fairly bad things like Type II diabetes are
>extremely well established at this point.
...
>In short, I'd say that at the very least you should not ignore the advice
>about diet completely. Even if veganism is a bit contraversial, lowering
>your intake of fat and trying to eat a balanced diet is now almost certainly
>demonstrated to be a good thing.

One problem with interpreting general advice is that I don't know how
much fat and fibre I eat, relative to the recommended levels, or what
those recommended levels might be.  Exhortations to the general
population to eat less of this or more of that are pretty meaningless
for individual cases.  The ultimate absurdity I once saw was a poster
that bluntly stated "Everyone eats too much salt".  No matter how much I
might cut down my salt intake, that poster is still there telling me to
cut it down even more.  More practical advice might be something like
"Throw away your salt cellar, you get plenty salt in your food anyway".

Of course, eating and drinking whatever I want doesn't mean bingeing on
junk food and getting plastered every weekend.  I consume almost no milk
products and drink no alcohol, because they disagree with me.  I enjoy
cuts of meat that include large lumps of fat, but the choice at the
cafeteria here and at local takeaways, and my disinclination to cook,
mean that I don't have that very often.  I eat brown bread, fresh fruit,
and a quarter to half a pound of meat a day.  No dietary supplements. 
Fortunately, I am blessed with a metabolism that doesn't require me to
deliberately control my weight, which of itself has stayed a constant
125-130 lb. for years.  So apart perhaps from fat intake, I probably eat
a reasonable diet already, simply by trusting whatever my body seems to
be telling me.

